1. One-sentence summary  
   The code incrementally builds and updates two sequences of counts over all achievable partial sums of the input values in order to compute a final modular combinatorial quantity.

2. Core algorithmic approach  
   – A 0/1-knapsack (subset-sum) style dynamic programming in which, for each new value `a`, you iterate backwards over current sums to “convolve” the DP arrays by shifting and scaling;  
   – Modular arithmetic throughout, including maintaining a rolling power-of-3 multiplier.

3. Reusable components  
   1. Reverse-sweep DP update for subset-sum or polynomial convolution:  
      for i from sumDownTo0:  
        dp[i+a] += dp[i];  
        dp[i] *= factor;  
   2. Parallel DP arrays pattern when you need to track two related statistics (here `d[]` and `e[]`) with different update rules on the same index set.  
   3. Rolling exponentiation/modular multiplication to accumulate a global factor (e.g. `z *= 3; z %= M`) in sync with DP transitions.
